ype">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ahiram sarcophagus is a carved limestone coffin from Byblos, Lebanon, generally dated about 1000 BCE, though the date is disputed, and found in 1923 by the French…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahlamu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahlamu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahlamu</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ahlamu were nomadic groups of Syria and Mesopotamia named in cuneiform sources from the 14th century BCE, including the Amarna letters and Kassite letters from…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahlat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahlat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahlat</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahlat is a town on the north-western shore of Lake Van in eastern Turkey, known in medieval sources as Khilat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahluwalia-misl"><span class="enc-name">Ahluwalia misl</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ahluwalia misl was one of the twelve Sikh confederacies of 18th-century Punjab, led by Jassa Singh Ahluwalia, who also commanded the Dal Khalsa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-al-araj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Araj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Araj was the first Saadi sultan of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-al-mansur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Mansur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Mansur was the Saadi sultan of Morocco from 1578 to 1603, enriched by his victory over the Portuguese at Alcácer Quibir.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mukarram"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Mukarram</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Mukarram was the second ruler of the Sulayhid dynasty of Yemen, succeeding his father Ali al-Sulayhi after Ali was killed by the Najahids in the 1060s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Muqtadir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Muqtadir (died 1081) was the Hudid king of the Taifa of Zaragoza from 1046 and its most powerful ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mustain-ii"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Mustain II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Mustain II (died 1110) was the Hudid king of the Taifa of Zaragoza from 1085, the last of his line to rule the city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-alaq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Alaq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Alaq, also called Sultan Ahmad Khan, was a Chagatai khan of the eastern part of Moghulistan from 1487, a son of Yunus Khan, ruling from Turpan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-baba-al-timbukti"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Baba al-Timbukti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Baba al-Timbukti was a celebrated scholar and jurist of Timbuktu in the late sixteenth and early seventeenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-bakhsh-khan"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Bakhsh Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Bakhsh Khan was the first Nawab of Loharu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-bin-rashid-al-mualla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-bin-rashid-al-mualla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad bin Rashid Al Mualla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad bin Rashid Al Mualla was ruler of Umm al-Quwain, one of the Trucial States, from 1929 to 1981.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-fadl-al-qumandan"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan was a prince of the Abdali ruling house of the Sultanate of Lahej in southern Arabia, a poet and musician who also commanded the sultanate's…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-fanakati"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Fanakati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Fanakati was a Central Asian Muslim from Banakat who served as chief finance minister of the Yuan dynasty under Kublai Khan for some twenty years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-faris-al-shidyaq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-faris-al-shidyaq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Faris al-Shidyaq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Faris al-Shidyaq was an Ottoman scholar, writer and journalist from Mount Lebanon, considered one of the founding fathers of modern Arabic literature.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-i-bey"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad I Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad I Bey was the Husainid bey of Tunis from 1837 to 1855.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-abd-al-aziz-ibn-abi-dulaf"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Dulaf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz was the most notable ruler of the Dulafid dynasty, which governed the region of Jibal in western Iran under the Abbasid caliphs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Fadlan was an Arab member of the embassy sent by the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir to the king of the Volga Bulgars in 921 to 922.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-furtu"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Furtu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Furtu was the chief imam and chronicler of Mai Idris Alooma of the Bornu Empire in the late sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hanbal"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Hanbal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Hanbal was an Arab jurist and hadith scholar of Baghdad, the founder of the Hanbali school of Sunni law and the compiler of al-Musnad, the largest hadith…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hasan-maymandi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Hasan Maymandi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Hasan Maymandi was a Persian administrator who served as vizier to the Ghaznavid sultan Mahmud of Ghazni from about 1013 to 1024.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi, known as Ahmad Gragn, was the imam and military leader of the Adal Sultanate who led a jihad against the Ethiopian Empire from 1527 to 1543.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-idris"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Idris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi was a Moroccan Sufi teacher whose reformist teaching stressed the direct study of the Quran and hadith.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-said-al-busaidi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Said al-Busaidi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Said al-Busaidi was the founder of the Al Bu Said dynasty of Oman.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Tulun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Tulun was a Turkish soldier sent to govern Egypt for the Abbasids in 868 who made himself its independent ruler and founded the Tulunid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-yahya-al-yahsubi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi was the founder of the Taifa of Niebla in south-western al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-jalayir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Jalayir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Jalayir was the Jalayirid sultan of Baghdad and Tabriz from 1382 to 1410, a poet, calligrapher and patron of manuscript painting.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khan-bangash"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ahmad-kh